1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:04,657
The final event in the diving pool is the men's 10 meter platform competition.

2
00:00:05,057 --> 00:00:07,320
One of the blue ribbon events of the whole games.

3
00:00:07,782 --> 00:00:13,228
A spectacular combination of artistry, athleticism and sheer courage.

4
00:00:13,849 --> 00:00:15,852
It's a long journey to the medals podium though.

5
00:00:15,872 --> 00:00:20,039
32 divers are in the pool for tonight's preliminary round.

6
00:00:20,539 --> 00:00:24,405
Only 18 will survive to make it into tomorrow morning's semi-final.

7
00:00:24,765 --> 00:00:29,532
The top 12 from there will go forward to tomorrow night to compete for gold.

8
00:00:30,068 --> 00:00:44,978
Expect the atmosphere to hit fever pitch tonight because Great Britain's poster boy, Tom Daly, begins his personal quest for glory, having missed out on a medal in the 10m Cinco competition that he and Peter Waterfield finished just out of the frame in fourth place.

9
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:48,887
The Brits are in action tonight and they've brought their supporters ever.

10
00:00:49,323 --> 00:00:51,606
But the world's best are all in the line-up here.

11
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:54,570
Two Olympic champions, two world champions.

12
00:00:55,149 --> 00:01:05,403
The Chinese have dominated in the diving pool again, but Russia's Ilya Zakharov, who won a gold at three metres, as we look at Tom Daley and Peter Waterfield there, they're the ones getting the cheers.

13
00:01:06,103 --> 00:01:09,789
Well, that Russian has proved the Chinese are not completely unbeatable.

14
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:15,596
Indeed, 10 metres is supposed to be the Chinese team's most vulnerable event, mind you.

15
00:01:15,896 --> 00:01:18,840
They've only won gold in three of the last five Olympic Games.

16
00:01:19,832 --> 00:01:27,817
We'll explain the scoring and some of the more technical aspects as we go along, but the important thing here is just to enjoy a great show.

17
00:01:28,203 --> 00:01:31,346
Steve Robiliotis alongside me looking to do just that as well.

18
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:37,474
Steve, you spent a lot of these games covering the gymnastics and in many ways is this what these guys are?

19
00:01:37,513 --> 00:01:38,736
They're gymnasts.

20
00:01:38,756 --> 00:01:45,864
Well they are really and you're going to see a lot of the skills from gymnastics transfer across to the 10 metre platform.

21
00:01:45,885 --> 00:01:56,977
They've only got three seconds though to get it done from platform to water or even a little less than that and six dives each in what is quite a lengthy session for them and as you mentioned

22
00:01:56,957 --> 00:02:02,230
It's a little bit of a marathon effort to get yourself from these preliminaries into the final.

23
00:02:02,992 --> 00:02:04,796
So they don't want to start poorly.

24
00:02:04,816 --> 00:02:07,203
In that respect it's also like the gymnastics.

25
00:02:07,222 --> 00:02:12,735
You have a major qualifying day which allows you to progress to a number of other events.

26
00:02:12,776 --> 00:02:14,460
In this case it's the

27
00:02:14,439 --> 00:02:29,496
platform on through to the semi-final to the final and we wish them all well and we wish them safe competition because that is a long way up and some of the competitors will tell you themselves they don't know why they do it and they're very aware of the danger but it also makes for spectacular sport.

28
00:02:29,509 --> 00:02:35,020
They hit the water at speeds in excess of 30 miles per hour, so get it wrong.

29
00:02:35,382 --> 00:02:45,983
And getting it wrong is the big pressure, of course, Steve, because, again, like the gymnasts and one or two of these artistic sports, that you cannot afford to get a dive wrong, because it could be game over.

30
00:02:45,962 --> 00:02:59,219
And with your first dive, there's always that pressure, no matter how experienced you might be, and Gleb Galperin will go up first, the 27-year-old Russian, and he's seen it all, done it all, been out for a spell with injuries like many of them have.

31
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:10,974
But to be the very first competitor up brings its own pressure, but it won't be unfamiliar to most of these lads who come to this level of competition only because they're very, very good.

32
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:13,300
Six dives each tonight.

33
00:03:13,461 --> 00:03:15,703
Each dive has an agreed degree of difficulty.

34
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:18,705
Seven judges who mark each dive out of 10.

35
00:03:19,146 --> 00:03:21,168
The highest and lowest two marks are discounted.

36
00:03:21,187 --> 00:03:27,373
Then the remaining total is multiplied by the degree of difficulty or the tariff that is given to each dive.

37
00:03:27,653 --> 00:03:28,534
Sounds complicated.

38
00:03:28,914 --> 00:03:30,156
The captions you'll see come out.

39
00:03:30,175 --> 00:03:33,378
The graphics there will make it very clear which marks are counting.

40
00:03:33,639 --> 00:03:36,322
So don't worry too much about any technicalities.

41
00:03:36,822 --> 00:03:41,907
Just enjoy the show, as we've said, because the action here can be spectacular.

42
00:03:41,887 --> 00:03:54,419
11 of these divers plus one more have already been in action in the synchronized swimming which at least gives them a feel of the pool but for most of them it's been a long long wait to get their Olympic Games underway.

43
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:04,128
Quite a warm evening and we're sitting up towards the top of the venue where the warm air is settling and we are certainly feeling it.

44
00:04:04,489 --> 00:04:08,112
I wouldn't mind a dip in that pool myself but I don't think they're going to let us down there Roger.

45
00:04:08,092 --> 00:04:09,495
Unfortunately not.

46
00:04:09,514 --> 00:04:12,598
You'll see there's just a little bit of a glaze across the top of the pool.

47
00:04:12,639 --> 00:04:22,050
They put a spray on top of it because when the divers are coming down it gets rid of that mirror effect and they can actually judge the distance they are from the water.

48
00:04:22,071 --> 00:04:24,533
I mean it's frightening to think all that is going through their mind.

49
00:04:24,754 --> 00:04:30,622
They tend to pick a spot on the ceiling or the outside to use as a rotation point to orientate themselves around.

50
00:04:31,543 --> 00:04:34,607
For those of us who don't do this sport, we don't know how they do it really.

51
00:04:34,586 --> 00:04:49,887
No, it takes a lot of courage, a serious amount of training and work in the gym and a bit of work between the ears as well to convince yourself how it should be done and that should it be done at all at Gleb Galperin first of all.

52
00:04:50,267 --> 00:04:55,735
One of the most experienced competitors in the competition, the dives will get more difficult as they go along.

53
00:04:58,482 --> 00:05:02,206
Inward three and a half, Somersault with a 3.2 tariff.

54
00:05:02,226 --> 00:05:04,170
And always so pleased to get that first one out of the way.

55
00:05:04,571 --> 00:05:06,593
And it looked like he did a very good job.

56
00:05:07,615 --> 00:05:09,536
In tight to the platform.

57
00:05:09,778 --> 00:05:13,202
That's part of the danger, of course, and hit the water nice and vertically.

58
00:05:13,242 --> 00:05:19,831
And the good score is coming in for him.

59
00:05:20,512 --> 00:05:22,935
And an 88 to start with.

60
00:05:22,956 --> 00:05:27,762
And four scores rejected, as Roger's explained to you, the top two and bottom two.

61
00:05:29,853 --> 00:05:32,036
Nine and nine and a halfs are excellent stuff.

62
00:05:32,096 --> 00:05:35,341
This is Lin Yue, who's one of the two Chinese divers.

63
00:05:36,103 --> 00:05:50,528
He was the 2008 Olympic Synchro champion competing this time individually in with three and a half somersault tucked position.

64
00:05:50,548 --> 00:05:56,096
Just managed to fold it in underneath the water, make that little somersault beneath to try and get those shins vertical.

65
00:05:59,839 --> 00:06:04,415
Nice tight tuck position and gets through those somersaults early so he can spot the water.

66
00:06:04,435 --> 00:06:10,574
20 of nines coming the way of Lin Yue.

67
00:06:12,630 --> 00:06:18,418
And they try and drag those bubbles down with them to create the best possible entry and least possible splash.

68
00:06:20,079 --> 00:06:25,427
You'll see them on the way down, the replays, which I should emphasize, the judges don't see those replays.

69
00:06:26,488 --> 00:06:30,473
We do, you do, but the judges have to get their judgment right in one go.

70
00:06:31,175 --> 00:06:39,464
Victor Ortega from Colombia, his second Olympic Games, and he kicks off for the back two and a half somersault, one and a half twist, piked.

71
00:06:44,608 --> 00:06:50,055
Oh just rolled that one over with his feet and that's a little error that the judges never miss.

72
00:06:54,298 --> 00:06:56,281
Certainly seem to get through the twisting okay.

73
00:06:56,661 --> 00:07:02,286
Spots the water now and those feet just roll over so no chance of nines there.

74
00:07:02,346 --> 00:07:12,016
The judges are looking for that tightness in either the tucked or the pikes position just how close you can get your torso to the thighs and really bunched up there.

75
00:07:13,499 --> 00:07:21,189
So, he's giving up a few points to the early divers already but the chances to make them up, of course, with this long first session.

76
00:07:25,935 --> 00:07:27,637
Anton Zakharov from Ukraine.

77
00:07:28,439 --> 00:07:28,918
Next to go.

78
00:07:28,939 --> 00:07:33,185
Not to be confused with Ilya Zakharov from Russia who won the Syncro competition.

79
00:07:36,168 --> 00:07:37,971
In with

80
00:07:41,865 --> 00:07:49,141
popular starting dive and it gives you a chance at home to compare how well it's performed.

81
00:07:51,225 --> 00:07:54,651
Closeness to the board is just unnerving at times, isn't it?

82
00:07:54,692 --> 00:07:58,660
Yeah, and I don't think he was still a little bent at the hips.

83
00:08:00,648 --> 00:08:01,668
as he entered the water.

84
00:08:01,749 --> 00:08:02,971
So it wasn't quite vertical.

85
00:08:04,773 --> 00:08:05,754
Hadn't quite come out of it.

86
00:08:05,774 --> 00:08:10,360
A little concern there, I think, for how tight he was to the platform.

87
00:08:10,961 --> 00:08:12,242
And seventy-two for Zakharov.

88
00:08:17,108 --> 00:08:19,490
Timofey from Belarus.

89
00:08:21,432 --> 00:08:30,064
Tenth in the World Championships in Shanghai in twenty-eleven as his best performance to date.

90
00:08:31,478 --> 00:08:32,039
point.

91
00:08:39,966 --> 00:09:06,868
and maybe just slightly under rotating as he hit the water as opposed to Ortega who overcooked it a little bit of a loss of leg form there as the ankles came apart so it sounds too like the judges have spotted what we have the ankles just couldn't quite lock themselves together they actually came apart twice so 72

92
00:09:10,948 --> 00:09:14,432
from the Democratic People's Republic of Korea.

93
00:09:14,953 --> 00:09:20,321
Just 16 years of age, there's a lot of teenagers in this competition as well.

94
00:09:27,950 --> 00:09:29,292
Forward three and a half somersault.

95
00:09:33,096 --> 00:09:39,504
Galperin's 88 is still standing at the moment, but we're expecting the Chinese competitors here to do a wonderful job.

96
00:09:41,729 --> 00:09:42,691
So graceful.

97
00:09:45,937 --> 00:09:48,582
And you see that he's absolutely ripped that entry.

98
00:09:48,702 --> 00:09:49,784
There's barely a splash.

99
00:09:55,836 --> 00:09:58,201
This drags all the bubbles down with him.

100
00:10:03,413 --> 00:10:04,855
two eights and an eight and a half there.

101
00:10:06,599 --> 00:10:09,544
This is Jimmy Connor from Australia.

102
00:10:10,386 --> 00:10:13,393
Seventeen years of age from Melbourne.

103
00:10:14,996 --> 00:10:16,679
Lives in Brisbane, trains there.

104
00:10:21,317 --> 00:10:23,240
And he's just rolled the feet over there.

105
00:10:23,279 --> 00:10:26,323
He won't be too pleased with that first effort.

106
00:10:26,344 --> 00:10:29,168
He was the youngest in the Australian team at the Delhi Commonwealth Games.

107
00:10:29,187 --> 00:10:30,009
He was just 15.

108
00:10:31,370 --> 00:10:33,413
He's come to the top quickly and early.

109
00:10:34,734 --> 00:10:38,759
But not perhaps his best opening dive as the sevens come in.

110
00:10:42,384 --> 00:10:43,544
He's entitled to be nervous.

111
00:10:43,794 --> 00:10:45,960
frighteningly young some of these competitors.

112
00:10:47,744 --> 00:11:01,533
Quite an age range right up into the early 30s like Peter Waterfield from Great Britain, one of the more senior divers in the whole competition down to 15 and 16 year old young boys really, Martin Wolfram.

113
00:11:01,514 --> 00:11:03,818
He's age 20 from Germany.

114
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:09,750
Arm stand back, double somersault, two and a half twists, an ambitious opening dive.

115
00:11:10,350 --> 00:11:14,700
His arm stand positions are really heart in mouth for all of us watching.

116
00:11:16,081 --> 00:11:18,086
High tariff to start off with at 3.6.

117
00:11:18,147 --> 00:11:20,451
See if he can cash in.

118
00:11:24,649 --> 00:11:31,706
Now okay, we consider the degree of difficulty is going to help him, but the execution not perfect.

119
00:11:36,023 --> 00:11:39,145
The lower tariff dives have got to be executed absolutely spot on.

120
00:11:39,166 --> 00:11:44,451
You've got a little bit more margin for error on the higher, more difficult dives, but obviously they want to cash in, don't they?

121
00:11:44,952 --> 00:11:46,534
Yeah, and a different sort of error there.

122
00:11:46,554 --> 00:11:47,936
He wasn't quite square to the water.

123
00:11:47,975 --> 00:11:50,918
His feet were twisted, ankles certainly together.

124
00:11:51,519 --> 00:11:55,904
And that, again, should be an error that the judges can catch at first look, their first and only look.

125
00:12:00,049 --> 00:12:05,034
Jankler Aguera from Cuba, second Olympic Games.

126
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:29,553
age 22 another forward three and a half somersault well he crashed into the water really that was hardly missable the error is on the entry he's a little bent in the pike position there's a few problems here for him he wasn't square

127
00:12:33,095 --> 00:12:35,460
And it is the errors that the judges are looking for.

128
00:12:35,701 --> 00:12:38,326
We certainly don't mean to sound negative about these great divers.

129
00:12:38,385 --> 00:12:40,750
They're at the Olympics because they're the world's best.

130
00:12:40,770 --> 00:12:44,099
But we're trying to reflect the thoughts of the judges here.

131
00:12:44,119 --> 00:12:47,346
And the 61.5, that's the lowest so far.

132
00:12:47,365 --> 00:12:48,488
So he had a few problems.

133
00:12:49,210 --> 00:13:03,166
Next up is Alexander Bondar from the Ukraine who's already competed in the Syncro competition with Alexander Kovarov finished eighth in with three and a half somersault to begin his routine.

134
00:13:12,076 --> 00:13:12,777
All very tidy.

135
00:13:13,878 --> 00:13:15,159
Should do well here, Bondar.

136
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:18,964
Not too many

137
00:13:23,787 --> 00:13:29,293
Some of the Eastern European divers are a little bit more powerfully built, which does tend to give a different impression.

138
00:13:29,313 --> 00:13:36,782
They're almost more muscular divers than some of the Chinese divers who are so incredibly slight and whippy through the air, aren't they?

139
00:13:37,442 --> 00:13:40,267
Yes, and there's less mass entering the water.

140
00:13:40,287 --> 00:13:44,831
It gives the Chinese divers a better entry quite often.

141
00:13:45,011 --> 00:13:49,437
But the powerful men can certainly get the job done in a different way.

142
00:13:50,418 --> 00:13:52,461
Jiho Park from Korea.

143
00:13:57,975 --> 00:14:04,203
Just rolling over a little for Park, out of Busan in Korea.

144
00:14:07,327 --> 00:14:12,134
Had a successful Asian Games, third in the Syncro three-meter springboard.

145
00:14:16,438 --> 00:14:17,841
And 72.

146
00:14:20,587 --> 00:14:22,029
Equals seventh best mark.

147
00:14:22,931 --> 00:14:28,958
Going to take a long time for these positions to really make a huge amount of sense with 32 divers to to get through.

148
00:14:28,999 --> 00:14:41,533
Really a question of them getting this evening out the way, making no mistakes, getting a feel for the competition and maybe at the top end laying down the odd marker if you're amongst those with high aspirations.

149
00:14:41,634 --> 00:14:43,918
Victor Minibayev from Russia.

150
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:48,283
Sixth in the 10 meter synchro as well.

151
00:14:50,440 --> 00:14:52,241
in with three and a half somersault tucked.

152
00:15:05,059 --> 00:15:12,870
Well he was there a long time and I wonder if there were a few stray thoughts entering his mind with the execution

153
00:15:14,452 --> 00:15:19,821
was solid until he kicked out for the entry and he was just caught a bit short.

154
00:15:21,443 --> 00:15:23,386
So that large splash is such a giveaway.

155
00:15:25,470 --> 00:15:27,033
A little bit of a split in the tuck position.

156
00:15:27,072 --> 00:15:28,173
Most of the divers have that.

157
00:15:28,214 --> 00:15:30,378
It doesn't upset the judges too much.

158
00:15:30,418 --> 00:15:32,181
They really only see that from side on.

159
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:36,326
But Minnibaev has paid a penalty for the entry for sure.

160
00:15:37,774 --> 00:15:44,503
Got to get the right push off as well to get through the rotations in time to unwind to give themselves the chance of the clean entry.

161
00:15:46,764 --> 00:15:47,846
There's no spring on that.

162
00:15:47,885 --> 00:15:52,211
Obviously, it all comes from their own push off and their own technique.

163
00:15:52,611 --> 00:15:54,153
German Sanchez from Mexico.

164
00:15:56,475 --> 00:15:58,197
Silver medalist in the 10 meter synchro.

165
00:15:58,477 --> 00:16:02,982
Mexico who've had a decent games in the diving pool.

166
00:16:04,304 --> 00:16:06,346
Such a strong nation in this sport, Mexico.

167
00:16:08,774 --> 00:16:13,144
Oh nice, the crowd really appreciated that entry there.

168
00:16:14,365 --> 00:16:23,846
They're instant experts like everyone at home but you learn to pick a good dive don't you and the entry is such an important impression to leave.

169
00:16:24,889 --> 00:16:26,052
They call it the rip, don't they?

170
00:16:26,072 --> 00:16:27,634
And you can hear that different sound.

171
00:16:28,176 --> 00:16:30,880
He's only 47km at Seven Stone Six.

172
00:16:31,240 --> 00:16:35,408
He's one of the lightest going and really the spin speed there in his rotations.

173
00:16:35,428 --> 00:16:36,931
And he's earned the top mark so far.

174
00:16:37,010 --> 00:16:44,804
Galperin, the first diver, was leading for quite a time there with 88, but now 89.1 for Jemin Sanchez.

175
00:16:45,881 --> 00:16:52,496
Well, his strong performance in the synchro would certainly have upped his confidence and confidence as in all sports, but this in particular is massive.

176
00:16:52,937 --> 00:16:55,625
And you can understand why from that formidable height.

177
00:16:56,245 --> 00:16:57,690
Riley McCormick from Kamina.

178
00:17:03,042 --> 00:17:05,207
Again, a very nice entry from Riley.

179
00:17:09,035 --> 00:17:10,416
his second Olympics.

180
00:17:10,436 --> 00:17:19,588
He didn't make the final in Beijing, finished 16th so his first aim will be to not just get through tonight but tomorrow morning and be in the final will be good for him.

181
00:17:19,608 --> 00:17:26,057
He'll turn 21 in a couple of weeks and has made a very solid start here.

182
00:17:26,077 --> 00:17:34,368
73.5 leaves him in equal seventh position and now lots of interest about our next competitor.

183
00:17:34,630 --> 00:17:36,392
Yep, one of the stars of the show

184
00:17:38,026 --> 00:17:40,587
The superstar from Beijing, Matthew Mitchum.

185
00:17:43,750 --> 00:17:45,231
He is a big man, isn't he?

186
00:17:46,894 --> 00:17:49,276
Well, he's a big star in Australia, that's for sure.

187
00:17:49,415 --> 00:17:54,059
His face has been all over the pre-Olympic publicity because he's a defending champion.

188
00:17:55,080 --> 00:18:01,767
But I think even Matthew would admit that the entry here wasn't quite what he was looking for.

189
00:18:02,508 --> 00:18:07,172
The ever so tiny over rotation of the feet.

190
00:18:10,797 --> 00:18:25,659
first Australian male to win an Olympic gold medal in diving since 1924 when he got it four years ago in Beijing in the most spectacular fashion with one of the great dives of all time and the last round to snatch victory.

191
00:18:29,124 --> 00:18:30,846
Loves competing, you can see that with a smile.

192
00:18:32,608 --> 00:18:35,573
Armand Hismervik of Norway.

193
00:18:41,476 --> 00:18:48,326
Opening back two and a half somersault with one and a half twists and he got through everything here pretty tidily.

194
00:18:50,028 --> 00:18:55,217
Feet just came together a little tidily but he's going to pick up some nice marks here.

195
00:18:56,298 --> 00:18:59,123
Gizmovic with an 81.6.

196
00:18:59,182 --> 00:19:05,353
Anything in the 80s is going to leave you in good stead for your second dive and onward.

197
00:19:05,373 --> 00:19:08,917
It's German Sanchez, huge first score of 89.1.

198
00:19:10,535 --> 00:19:13,459
Francesco Delomo from Italy.

199
00:19:13,499 --> 00:19:18,224
This is his third Olympic Games.

200
00:19:18,265 --> 00:19:19,747
So, an experienced competitor.

201
00:19:25,154 --> 00:19:27,678
Forward two and a half somersault with one twist, Pite.

202
00:19:28,699 --> 00:19:29,200
Very nice.

203
00:19:29,220 --> 00:19:30,862
Bring lots of experience to bear here.

204
00:19:30,882 --> 00:19:38,752
Ninth in the platform back in Athens, oh four.

205
00:19:46,259 --> 00:19:47,980
Not too much wrong there for Francesco.

206
00:19:48,040 --> 00:19:49,423
Just a confidence building start.

207
00:19:49,442 --> 00:19:50,203
72.5.

208
00:19:51,404 --> 00:19:52,526
We'll need to build on that.

209
00:19:54,509 --> 00:20:00,875
Varying degrees of pedigree in the country's diving history, really.

210
00:20:01,636 --> 00:20:09,926
Divers like Christopher Eskilsson from Sweden don't have a massive tradition in this sport and facilities sometimes more limited.

211
00:20:10,086 --> 00:20:14,932
Back two and a half somersault, one and a half twist for his opening dive.

212
